# Cursor Rules

You are an expert software engineer. Follow these rules strictly in every response.
- Always ask me question if you are not sure about the task or the code.
- don't assume anything, always ask me questions.

---

## Core Philosophy

- Prioritize simplicity over complexity
- Avoid overengineering
- Write clean, readable, maintainable code
- Follow modern best practices and up-to-date documentation
- Maintain consistent patterns across the codebase

---

## Terminal Rules

- Always provide commands for the terminal
- Never execute commands on behalf of the user

---

## Readability & Maintainability

- Code must be human-readable
- Use clear and descriptive naming
- Follow modern coding standards
- Keep logic easy to follow and maintain

---

## Comment Rules

- Never delete existing comments unless the related code is removed
- Always explain WHY, not WHAT
- Add comments for non-obvious decisions
- Keep comments concise and human-readable

---

## Naming Conventions

- Variables → camelCase
- Functions → camelCase
- Classes / Widgets → PascalCase
- Files → snake_case.dart

---

## Code Style

- Prefer small, simple functions
- Max function length: 40 lines
- Avoid deep nesting
- Avoid unnecessary abstraction
- Keep logic straightforward

---

## Development Rules

- Keep code consistent across the project
- Follow existing patterns before introducing new ones
- Use latest stable libraries and standards
- Avoid unnecessary dependencies
- Focus on maintainability and clarity

---

## Knowledge Base Rule

Maintain a file called `MyKnowledgeBase.txt` in the project root.

- If the file does not exist, create it
- add new significant insights learned that will be useful for future projects
- avoid adding very simple insights that can be easily found in the internet
- include insights that are not obvious and require thinking
- insights that you learn about the architectue e.g. CDK, Amplify, State management, etc.
```

Format the file like this:

-----Topic Title-----
Insight 1: 
Insight 2: 
Lesson learned: 

-----Topic Title-----
Insight 1: 
Insight 2: 
Lesson learned: 

-----Topic Title-----
Insight 1: 
Description: 
Insight 1: 
Insight 2: 
Lesson learned: 
Insight 2: 
Lesson learned: 
```

---

## Anti-Patterns

Never do the following:

- Overengineer simple features
- Create large monolithic classes
- Mix UI and business logic
- Delete comments unnecessarily
- Write unclear or overly abstract code

---

## Best Practices

Always do the following:

- Use descriptive naming
- Break code into logical components
- Keep files focused and small
- Follow consistent patterns
- Write code for future maintainers

---

## Project Structure

Use a feature-first architecture:

```
lib/
├── main.dart
│
├── app/
│   ├── app.dart
│   ├── router.dart
│   │
│   ├── theme/
│   │   ├── theme.dart
│   │   ├── light_theme.dart
│   │   └── dark_theme.dart
│   │
│   └── layout/
│       └── app_layout.dart
│
├── core/
│   ├── config/
│   ├── utils/
│   ├── widgets/
│   ├── services/
│   └── icons/
│
├── shared/
│   ├── models/
│   └── widgets/
│
└── features/
    ├── todo/
    │   ├── screens/
    │   ├── widgets/
    │   ├── services/
    │   ├── models/
    │   └── subscreens/
    │
    ├── event/
    ├── auth/
    ├── pomodoro/
    ├── guest/
    └── profile/
```

Each feature must contain:

- `screens/`
- `widgets/`
- `services/`
- `models/`
- `subscreens/`

---

## Architecture Rules

- Use feature-first structure
- Prefer modular and decoupled design
- When designing a UI for the first time, always use dummy data unless told otherwise

---

## Final Rule

Code should be easy to read, easy to change, and easy to scale.

If it feels complex — simplify it.
